{"title":"Career roadmap · Social Researcher","role":"Social Researcher","summary":"A Social Researcher studies people, communities, cultures, institutions and social behaviour using systematic research methods. The role can involve surveys, interviews, ethnographic observation, demographic analysis, community studies, public-policy research and qualitative/quantitative data analysis.","facts":{"Current degree":"B.Sc (Bachelor of Science – General / Honours)","Course":"Anthropology"},"about":[],"intro":[],"horizons":[],"sections":[{"heading":"Overview","kind":"other","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"For a B.Sc Anthropology student, this is a strongly aligned career, particularly for research in anthropology, sociology, development, public health, migration, tribal studies, gender, education, livelihoods and community development."},{"type":"p","text":"A bachelor's degree can support research assistant and field-research roles, while specialist research, university and senior policy positions commonly benefit from an M.A./M.Sc. and often a PhD."},{"type":"h","text":"Career facts"},{"type":"table","header":["Item","Details"],"rows":[[{"text":"Starts"},{"text":"Year 1"}],[{"text":"Current degree"},{"text":"B.Sc Anthropology – General / Honours"}],[{"text":"Desired career"},{"text":"Social Researcher"}],[{"text":"Career cluster"},{"text":"Social Science · Research · Development · Policy"}],[{"text":"Primary route"},{"text":"B.Sc Anthropology → Research Internship/Assistant → Social Researcher"}],[{"text":"Strong advantage"},{"text":"Anthropology + fieldwork + statistics + qualitative research"}],[{"text":"Higher qualification"},{"text":"M.A./M.Sc Anthropology, Sociology, Social Research, Development Studies or related field"}],[{"text":"Long-term research route"},{"text":"Master's → Researcher → PhD → Research Scientist/Academic/Policy Specialist"}]]},{"type":"h","text":"Career-route check"},{"type":"p","text":"Strongly aligned."},{"type":"p","text":"Anthropology is particularly useful because it combines the study of culture, society, human behaviour, communities and field-based research."},{"type":"p","text":"For research-intensive positions, build capability in both:"},{"type":"h","text":"Qualitative research + Quantitative research"},{"type":"p","text":"rather than relying only on theoretical anthropology."},{"type":"h","text":"Timeline assumption"},{"type":"p","text":"For a four-year B.Sc structure beginning in 2026, graduation would typically be around 2030. If the university follows a three-year B.Sc structure, compress the roadmap accordingly."}]},{"heading":"Career horizons","kind":"horizons","blocks":[{"type":"table","header":["Period","Career objective"],"rows":[[{"text":"2026–2030"},{"text":"Build anthropology fundamentals, fieldwork, research methodology, statistics and academic writing."}],[{"text":"2030–2035"},{"text":"Enter research-assistant/field-research roles or pursue a master's in anthropology, social research, sociology, development or public policy."}],[{"text":"2035–2040"},{"text":"Progress toward Social Researcher, Research Associate, Research Fellow, Policy Researcher or Social Scientist."}],[{"text":"2040–2046"},{"text":"Move toward Principal Researcher, Research Lead, Policy Specialist, Programme Evaluation Lead, academic or research-director roles."}]]}]},{"heading":"What to build each year","kind":"build","blocks":[{"type":"table","header":["When","Build this","Learn from","Proof of progress","Gap it moves"],"rows":[[{"text":"Year 1 – Sem 1"},{"text":"Anthropology fundamentals and human evolution"},{"text":"University + anthropology resources"},{"text":"Concept notes"},{"text":"Foundational knowledge"}],[{"text":"Year 1 – Sem 2"},{"text":"Cultural/social anthropology"},{"text":"Field observation"},{"text":"Community observation report"},{"text":"Social understanding"}],[{"text":"Year 2 – Sem 1"},{"text":"Research methodology"},{"text":"Research-methods coursework"},{"text":"Research proposal"},{"text":"